Rc<RefCell<T>> is for single-threaded shared ownership with interior mutability: multiple owners can borrow the same value, and mutation happens at runtime via RefCell's borrow rules. It is not thread-safe.

Arc<Mutex<T>> is for multi-threaded shared ownership: Arc provides atomic reference counting, and Mutex provides exclusive mutable access across threads. It is thread-safe but has synchronization overhead.

Use Rc<RefCell<T>> for single-threaded graphs/trees or shared state within one thread. Use Arc<Mutex<T>> when data is shared across threads.

Rc<RefCell<T>> example:
```rust
use std::rc::Rc;
use std::cell::RefCell;

let shared = Rc::new(RefCell::new(5));
let clone = Rc::clone(&shared);
*clone.borrow_mut() += 1;
println!("{}", shared.borrow());
```

Arc<Mutex<T>> example:
```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::thread;

let shared = Arc::new(Mutex::new(5));
let clone = Arc::clone(&shared);

thread::spawn(move || {
    *clone.lock().unwrap() += 1;
});

thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(10));
println!("{}", *shared.lock().unwrap());
```

